WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
A student needs the pH of zero point

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.040
one five zero moles per litre propanoic acid.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.440
They treat it like hydrochloric acid: the hydronium

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.480
equals the concentration, so the pH is zero

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.320
point eight two four.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.560
But the pH meter reads two point eight

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.440
five.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.240
That's two pH units out, about a hundred

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.400
times too much hydronium.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.320
So what went wrong, and how do you

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.000
calculate the right answer?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.880
Propanoic acid is weak.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.600
At any moment only a small fraction of

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.240
its molecules have given a proton to water,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.120
and the rest stay whole, so the hydronium

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.680
is far less than the acid's concentration.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.360
Molecules and ions sit in equilibrium, and you've

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.800
already written its constant, K a: hydronium times

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.680
propanoate, over the whole acid, with water left

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.640
out.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.560
So the real question is how much of

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.560
the acid ionises.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.080
Call that amount x.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.780
Here's the full method, with K a for

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.500
propanoic acid one point three times ten to

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:03.540
the minus five.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.020
An ICE table tracks the concentrations: initial, change,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:08.980
equilibrium.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.620
At the start there's zero point one five

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.340
zero of the acid and, ignoring water's own

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.540
tiny share, no ions.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.860
Each molecule that ionises makes one hydronium and

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.740
one propanoate, so the acid goes down by

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.660
x and each ion goes up by x.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.820
At equilibrium, that's zero point one five zero

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.700
minus x, then x, and x.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.420
So K a equals x squared over zero

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.820
point one five zero minus x.

00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:40.401
K a is tiny next to zero point

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:41.393
one five zero,

00:01:41.914 --> 00:01:43.393
so x is tiny too.

00:01:43.954 --> 00:01:46.353
Taking a tiny amount from zero point one

00:01:46.514 --> 00:01:49.233
five zero still leaves about zero point one

00:01:49.314 --> 00:01:51.393
five zero, so we drop the x from

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:53.793
the bottom, just like the tiny K shortcut

00:01:53.874 --> 00:01:55.113
in Module 5.

00:01:55.394 --> 00:01:57.913
Then x squared is K a times the

00:01:57.954 --> 00:02:00.833
concentration, one point nine five times ten to

00:02:00.874 --> 00:02:03.553
the minus six, and x is its square

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.953
root: one point four times ten to the

00:02:06.114 --> 00:02:07.873
minus three moles per litre.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.340
Now check that dropping x was fair.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.460
x is zero point nine three percent of

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.340
zero point one five zero, well under the

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:18.700
five percent rule of thumb.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.940
So at equilibrium, hydronium and propanoate are both

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.100
one point four times ten to the minus

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.660
three, and the acid is still about zero

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.220
point one four nine.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.380
The pH is two point eight five: x

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.940
has only two significant figures, because K a

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.300
does, so two decimal places.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.820
That matches the meter.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.260
Fewer than one molecule in a hundred has

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.740
ionised, which is why the strong acid method

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.100
was so far off.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.820
The shortcut isn't always safe.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.980
Hydrofluoric acid has a much bigger K a,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.540
and at zero point one zero zero moles

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.100
per litre, x comes out at about eight

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.180
percent of the concentration.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.900
That's over five percent, so dropping x would

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.300
change the answer too much, and you'd solve

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.420
the full equation instead, with the quadratic formula

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.740
or your calculator's solver.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.300
Most HSC questions are written so the check

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:15.100
passes, but you still show the check.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.780
Weak bases work the same way, with K

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.140
b and hydroxide.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.060
Ammonia takes protons from water, giving ammonium and

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.540
hydroxide, but only a little of it reacts.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.300
For zero point two zero zero moles per

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.780
litre ammonia, with K b one point eight

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.340
times ten to the minus five, the table

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.980
is the same: the ammonia goes down by

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.100
x, and ammonium and hydroxide each go up

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.300
by x.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.540
Drop the x, and x is the square

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.180
root of K b times c, one point

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.580
nine times ten to the minus three.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.620
That's under one percent, so the check passes.

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:56.873
But this time x is the hydroxide, not

00:03:56.954 --> 00:03:57.673
hydronium.

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:00.153
So, as in the strong acid and base

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:03.833
video, take the pOH first: two point seven

00:04:03.954 --> 00:04:04.633
two.

00:04:04.754 --> 00:04:07.753
Then the pH is fourteen minus that, eleven

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:09.193
point two eight.

00:04:09.394 --> 00:04:11.513
Writing two point seven two as the pH

00:04:11.594 --> 00:04:14.233
is the slip to avoid: a base can't

00:04:14.314 --> 00:04:15.833
have an acidic pH.

00:04:17.034 --> 00:04:20.313
So every pH question starts with one decision.

00:04:20.714 --> 00:04:21.513
Is it strong?

00:04:22.074 --> 00:04:24.633
Then the ions come straight from the concentration.

00:04:25.114 --> 00:04:25.833
Is it weak?

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:28.473
Then only a little reacts, so you need

00:04:28.554 --> 00:04:30.713
K a or K b and the ICE

00:04:30.794 --> 00:04:31.273
table.

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:33.273
A question that gives you a K a

00:04:33.354 --> 00:04:35.593
value is usually telling you the acid is

00:04:35.674 --> 00:04:36.153
weak.

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:38.926
So here's what to remember.

00:04:39.207 --> 00:04:41.206
Strong or weak first.

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:43.366
For a weak acid, x is about the

00:04:43.447 --> 00:04:46.246
square root of K a times c, then

00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:48.166
check that x is under five percent of

00:04:48.327 --> 00:04:49.046
c.

00:04:49.127 --> 00:04:50.966
And for a weak base, x is the

00:04:51.047 --> 00:04:54.886
hydroxide, so finish with fourteen minus pOH.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:56.740
Your turn.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.020
Methanoic acid is the acid in some ant

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:00.860
stings.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.860
Calculate the pH of a zero point two

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.220
zero zero moles per litre methanoic acid solution

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:10.420
at twenty five degrees, given that K a

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.380
is one point eight times ten to the

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.780
minus four.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.540
Show that the simplifying assumption is valid.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:18.100
Pause here to answer.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.020
Methanoic acid is weak, so drop the x:

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.900
x squared is K a times zero point

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.020
two zero zero, so x is six point

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:40.300
zero times ten to the minus three.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.860
That's three percent of the concentration, under five,

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.740
so the assumption is valid.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.580
The pH is two point two two.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.300
The full equation gives two point two three,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.540
only point zero one away.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.020
Treating the acid as strong gives zero point

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.220
six nine nine.

00:05:58.434 --> 00:05:59.073
Last one.

00:05:59.354 --> 00:06:02.273
Methylamine is a weak base that reacts with

00:06:02.354 --> 00:06:04.993
water in the same way as ammonia.

00:06:05.274 --> 00:06:07.553
Calculate the pH of a zero point four

00:06:07.714 --> 00:06:10.753
zero zero moles per litre methylamine solution at

00:06:10.834 --> 00:06:13.953
twenty five degrees, given that K b is

00:06:14.034 --> 00:06:15.953
four point four times ten to the minus

00:06:16.034 --> 00:06:16.913
four.

00:06:17.074 --> 00:06:18.273
Pause here to answer.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:32.540
Methylamine takes a proton from water, making hydroxide.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.580
x squared is K b times zero point

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.340
four zero zero, so x, the hydroxide, is

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.500
one point three times ten to the minus

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.940
two, about three percent, so the check passes.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.060
The pOH is one point eight eight, and

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.460
the pH is twelve point one two.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.380
Using zero point four zero zero as the

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:56.620
hydroxide treats a weak base as strong.

00:06:57.834 --> 00:07:01.593
So for any pH question, decide first: strong

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:02.473
or weak.

00:07:02.954 --> 00:07:05.033
Strong, use the concentration.

00:07:05.514 --> 00:07:08.873
Weak, write the equation, build the ICE table,

00:07:09.034 --> 00:07:10.873
drop x if K a or K b

00:07:10.954 --> 00:07:13.913
is tiny, check it's under five percent, then

00:07:13.994 --> 00:07:16.393
take the negative log, through pOH for a

00:07:16.474 --> 00:07:16.873
base.

00:07:17.994 --> 00:07:20.833
Next: why neutralising a weak acid can release

00:07:20.874 --> 00:07:22.313
a different amount of heat.

00:07:23.534 --> 00:07:24.973
Here are the key notes.

00:07:25.214 --> 00:07:27.133
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:27.214 --> 00:07:28.013
to copy them.
